// NESTED_IF_WORKAROUND (FORCED): its own take must not sit inside a branch.
// Revert scope for the in-flight compiler fix — grep NESTED_IF_WORKAROUND.
//
// wp_flusher_tick — the SLA sweep, and THE ONLY PLACE THE DURABLE WATERMARK
// ADVANCES.
//
// AXVERITY_EXTENT_WRITE_PATH 5d. slablock's handles are THREAD-LOCAL by design
// (the shared-nothing property it inherits from logbuf.rs), so slab_append_raw
// and slab_tick must be called by whichever thread called slab_open. The
// flusher is that thread, which is why a tick travels the whole queue to reach
// it rather than being actioned where it is born.
//
// slab_tick is SELF-GATED: if less than sla_us has elapsed it does nothing and
// returns -1. So calling it once per tick message is right even when the queue
// coalesced several — the gate, not the caller, controls fsync cadence. One
// sweep fsyncs EVERY block with unflushed data and seals every full one, which
// is the group commit the coalescing exists for.
//
// THE WATERMARK MOVES HERE BECAUSE THIS IS WHERE DURABILITY HAPPENS.
// wp_flusher_eject only appends — slab_append_raw never fsyncs — so it records
// the highest seq APPENDED and nothing more. A sweep that returns >= 0 has
// fsynced everything appended before it, so that is the moment the appended
// mark becomes a durable one. A GATED sweep (-1) has done nothing, and must
// not promote anything: the next tick will.
fn wp_flusher_tick(_u: Unit) -> Text {
  let _drop = bchan_take(Text("wp-flush-live"))
  let hs    = bchan_take(Text("wp-slab-s"))
  let _ps   = bchan_send(Text("wp-slab-s"), hs)
  let hp    = bchan_take(Text("wp-slab-p"))
  let _pp   = bchan_send(Text("wp-slab-p"), hp)

  let ts    = slab_tick(hs)
  let tp    = slab_tick(hp)

  // Fired if EITHER handle swept. A gated handle simply had nothing due.
  let fired = bool_or(int_gte(ts, Int(0)), int_gte(tp, Int(0)))
  let _prom = if fired { wp_promote_durable(Unit()) } else { io_print(Text("")) }
  str_concat(Text("0"), Text(""))
}
